WEBVTT

00:00:05.306 --> 00:00:10.746
Imagine half of your state's population just, you know, losing their healthcare funding in

00:00:10.746 --> 00:00:15.866
exactly 12 months. Right, yeah. I mean, not 13 months, not two years, exactly 12 months.

00:00:15.866 --> 00:00:18.826
Which is a terrifyingly short amount of time in government, honestly.

00:00:18.826 --> 00:00:24.266
Seriously. The hospitals lose their funding, the doctors face these drastic pay cuts,

00:00:24.986 --> 00:00:31.146
and the most vulnerable patients are suddenly staring at this massive gap in their coverage.

00:00:31.146 --> 00:00:35.146
And that's exactly the ticking clock Puerto Rico is facing right now.

00:00:35.146 --> 00:00:40.346
Welcome to this deep dive. Today we are bringing you into this highly complex,

00:00:40.346 --> 00:00:46.746
incredibly high stakes legislative roadmap. We really are. Our mission today is to unpackage

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:51.306
exactly what it will take to secure permanent federal Medicaid funding for Puerto Rico,

00:00:51.306 --> 00:00:55.146
and, you know, hopefully avoid this massive looming fiscal cliff.

00:00:55.146 --> 00:00:59.706
Yeah, and the stakes here, they really just cannot be overstated. We've got a whole stack

00:00:59.706 --> 00:01:03.066
of materials we are analyzing for you today. A lot to get through, right?

00:01:03.066 --> 00:01:08.906
Oh, absolutely. We're looking at congressional research service reports by Allison Mitchell,

00:01:09.626 --> 00:01:16.666
Puerto Rico's executive order 2026006, the September 2026 Medicaid financing roadmap,

00:01:16.666 --> 00:01:20.106
and a few reports from local agencies like ACES. Right.

00:01:20.106 --> 00:01:27.066
And they all point to one undeniable reality. We are talking about the health coverage of 1.3

00:01:27.066 --> 00:01:30.346
million beneficiaries on the planned vital system. Wow.

00:01:30.346 --> 00:01:35.146
Yeah. That is roughly 47% of Puerto Rico's entire population.

00:01:35.146 --> 00:01:37.466
So almost half the island. Exactly. Half the island.

00:01:37.466 --> 00:01:39.546
And as I mentioned, we have this strict,

00:01:39.546 --> 00:01:43.786
rabidly closing 12 month window to get this done in Congress.

00:01:43.786 --> 00:01:47.866
So we're basically going to walk you through this historic 12 months spread step by step.

00:01:47.866 --> 00:01:50.346
Because that's the only way to really understand the gravity of it.

00:01:50.346 --> 00:01:52.986
But, you know, before we actually get to the path forward,

00:01:52.986 --> 00:01:54.506
we have to look at the baseline facts.

00:01:54.666 --> 00:01:55.546
The foundation.

00:01:55.546 --> 00:01:59.306
Because in Washington, if you don't agree on the basic facts of a situation,

00:01:59.306 --> 00:02:02.186
you spend all your legislative time arguing about the bath,

00:02:02.186 --> 00:02:04.506
right, instead of debating the actual policy.

00:02:04.506 --> 00:02:08.186
Which happens way too often. And what's fascinating here is that

00:02:08.186 --> 00:02:13.226
this is precisely why Allison Mitchell's documents are so foundational to this entire effort.

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:16.826
So break that down for us. Who is Allison Mitchell?

00:02:16.826 --> 00:02:21.546
So she is a specialist in healthcare financing at the Congressional Research Service,

00:02:21.546 --> 00:02:23.866
or CRS for short. Okay.

00:02:23.866 --> 00:02:31.306
Her products specifically this two page staff brief, they call IF1-1012.

00:02:31.306 --> 00:02:38.106
And this comprehensive legislative history known as R47-601 these serve as the absolute

00:02:38.106 --> 00:02:39.786
gold standard for Congress.

00:02:39.786 --> 00:02:42.346
Wait, really just two reports are that influential?

00:02:42.346 --> 00:02:45.466
Yeah, because CRS does not lobby. They don't advocate for anyone.

00:02:45.466 --> 00:02:48.826
They provide strictly nonpartisan factual descriptions of current law.

00:02:48.826 --> 00:02:49.706
It's just the facts.

00:02:49.706 --> 00:02:50.826
Okay, let's unpack this.

00:02:50.826 --> 00:02:55.306
Think of the CRS like a referees rule book in a heated championship game.

00:02:55.306 --> 00:02:56.266
I like that, yeah.

00:02:56.266 --> 00:02:59.786
In Washington, if a Democrat and a Republican are arguing over a foul,

00:02:59.786 --> 00:03:02.586
I don't ask each other what the rules are. They just pull out the Mitchell report.

00:03:02.586 --> 00:03:06.826
Exactly. It instantly ends the math debate so they can get back to the actual policy fight.

00:03:06.826 --> 00:03:10.826
Right. Which makes having this nonpartisan baseline super crucial

00:03:10.826 --> 00:03:12.586
for this upcoming 12 month battle.

00:03:12.586 --> 00:03:17.146
But what does that rule book actually say about the cliff Puerto Rico is facing?

00:03:17.146 --> 00:03:20.026
Well, it paints a very stark picture of the expiration dates.

00:03:20.586 --> 00:03:24.586
Mitchell documents that Puerto Rico's current federal medical assistance percentage.

00:03:24.586 --> 00:03:25.786
Wait, the FMP?

00:03:25.786 --> 00:03:31.226
Yeah, the FMAP, which is essentially just the share of Medicaid costs the federal government pays.

00:03:31.786 --> 00:03:33.786
Right now, it's currently sitting at 76%.

00:03:34.346 --> 00:03:36.506
Okay, 76%. Sounds decent.

00:03:36.506 --> 00:03:38.266
It is, but here's the catch.

00:03:38.266 --> 00:03:43.626
That 76% rate along with these elevated funding allotments that were set for fiscal years

00:03:43.626 --> 00:03:49.066
2023 through 2027 completely expires on September 30th, 2027.

00:03:49.146 --> 00:03:50.586
So exactly 12 months from now.

00:03:50.586 --> 00:03:53.546
If Congress just, I don't know, sits on its hands and does nothing.

00:03:53.546 --> 00:03:58.106
If Congress does nothing, the funding for fiscal year 2028 is drastically recalculated

00:03:58.106 --> 00:04:00.346
based on this much lower 2019 baseline.

00:04:00.346 --> 00:04:00.986
Oh, wow.

00:04:00.986 --> 00:04:05.866
Yeah. And furthermore, that 76% FMAP plummets to a statutory 55%.

00:04:06.426 --> 00:04:09.546
A drop from 76 to 55%, that's huge.

00:04:09.546 --> 00:04:10.666
It's devastating.

00:04:10.666 --> 00:04:12.826
And to put that disparity in perspective,

00:04:12.826 --> 00:04:17.466
Mitchell's report clearly notes that the other four US territories already enjoy

00:04:17.466 --> 00:04:20.906
a permanent 83% FMAP.

00:04:20.906 --> 00:04:23.626
Wait, the other territories have a permanent 83%?

00:04:23.626 --> 00:04:24.426
Exactly.

00:04:24.426 --> 00:04:29.226
And without Mitchell's clear nonpartisan documentation establishing these facts,

00:04:29.226 --> 00:04:33.946
lawmakers would just completely waste this precious 12-month window

00:04:33.946 --> 00:04:35.946
debating whether the cliff even exists.

00:04:35.946 --> 00:04:39.706
Okay, so the referee's rulebook establishes that the cliff is very real

00:04:39.706 --> 00:04:41.706
and the disparity is heavily documented.

00:04:41.706 --> 00:04:42.826
Absolutely.

00:04:42.826 --> 00:04:46.666
So now that the legislative baseline is set by the CRS reports,

00:04:46.666 --> 00:04:50.826
how does Puerto Rico prove to Congress that they're actually ready for a permanent

00:04:50.826 --> 00:04:52.666
state-like funding structure?

00:04:52.666 --> 00:04:55.226
I mean, Congress isn't just going to hand over billions of dollars

00:04:55.226 --> 00:04:56.986
without some serious assurances, right?

00:04:56.986 --> 00:04:58.666
Well, they do it by showing their receipts.

00:04:58.666 --> 00:05:00.026
Like proving they've done the work.

00:05:00.026 --> 00:05:00.826
Exactly.

00:05:00.826 --> 00:05:05.786
The upcoming 2027 fight is essentially a continuation of the 2022 deal.

00:05:05.786 --> 00:05:10.026
To earn a permanent fix, Puerto Rico was assigned this extensive list of homework

00:05:10.026 --> 00:05:12.106
in the Consolidated Appropriations Act.

00:05:12.106 --> 00:05:14.106
The CAA of 2023.

00:05:14.106 --> 00:05:16.266
Right, the CAA of 2023.

00:05:16.666 --> 00:05:20.906
They had to rigorously comply with federal requirements over the last few years.

00:05:20.906 --> 00:05:21.626
And did they?

00:05:21.626 --> 00:05:22.586
They did.

00:05:22.586 --> 00:05:26.826
And the sources show that this compliance is arguably the strongest weapon

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:28.906
Puerto Rico currently brings to the table.

00:05:28.906 --> 00:05:32.986
So I know one of those homework assignments was raising the physician payment floor, right?

00:05:32.986 --> 00:05:34.186
That was a huge one, yeah.

00:05:34.746 --> 00:05:39.546
They successfully raised the physician payment floor to 75% of the Puerto Rico

00:05:39.546 --> 00:05:40.986
Medicare Part B rate.

00:05:40.986 --> 00:05:41.466
Okay.

00:05:41.466 --> 00:05:45.546
And they also secured the annual provider add-on of $300 million.

00:05:46.266 --> 00:05:49.866
Let me stop you there because I think we need to explain why that payment floor

00:05:49.866 --> 00:05:51.626
matters so much to the average listener.

00:05:51.626 --> 00:05:52.346
Sure, yeah.

00:05:52.346 --> 00:05:57.546
Before this, doctors on the island were vastly underpaid compared to the mainland.

00:05:57.546 --> 00:06:00.826
By raising that floor, they are actively trying to stop the brain drain, right?

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:01.386
Exactly.

00:06:01.386 --> 00:06:04.346
They're giving doctors a real financial reason to stay in Puerto Rico

00:06:04.346 --> 00:06:07.546
rather than just, you know, packing up and moving to Florida.

00:06:07.546 --> 00:06:10.746
It directly impacts the retention of medical professionals,

00:06:11.306 --> 00:06:13.386
which of course stabilizes the whole system.

00:06:13.946 --> 00:06:18.026
But the compliance goes much deeper into administrative integrity too.

00:06:18.026 --> 00:06:19.626
Oh, like what else did they do?

00:06:19.626 --> 00:06:22.426
Well, they implemented the asset verification system,

00:06:22.426 --> 00:06:26.026
which CMS, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services,

00:06:26.026 --> 00:06:29.946
officially approved to go into effect on January 1st, 2026.

00:06:29.946 --> 00:06:31.946
And what does that system actually do?

00:06:31.946 --> 00:06:36.586
It actively checks to ensure that people receiving benefits are genuinely eligible

00:06:36.586 --> 00:06:40.666
by verifying their financial assets, you know, which cuts down on fraud.

00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:42.346
Right, making sure the money goes where it's supposed to.

00:06:42.346 --> 00:06:43.066
Exactly.

00:06:43.066 --> 00:06:47.466
And they also designated both of the required program integrity leads.

00:06:47.466 --> 00:06:52.426
And I have to say, all of that administrative work leads to the statistic

00:06:52.426 --> 00:06:55.626
that absolutely blew my mind when I was reading through the sources.

00:06:55.626 --> 00:06:58.906
We have to talk about the payment error rate measurement, the PRM.

00:06:58.906 --> 00:07:00.506
Oh yes, the PRM.

00:07:00.506 --> 00:07:03.786
It is basically the ultimate report card for Medicaid efficiency.

00:07:03.786 --> 00:07:04.986
So give us the numbers.

00:07:04.986 --> 00:07:07.626
Well, the national average error rate for Medicaid payments

00:07:07.626 --> 00:07:10.026
across the United States is 5.019%.

00:07:10.746 --> 00:07:14.026
Puerto Rico achieved a PRM of just 2.51%.

00:07:14.586 --> 00:07:17.386
Wait, 2.51%, are you serious?

00:07:17.386 --> 00:07:18.106
I am.

00:07:18.106 --> 00:07:20.026
Less than half the national average.

00:07:20.026 --> 00:07:24.746
Anyone who has ever dealt with a massive government bureaucracy

00:07:24.746 --> 00:07:28.426
knows how astonishing it is to get an error rate that low.

00:07:28.426 --> 00:07:32.186
I mean, it completely defies typical bureaucratic expectations.

00:07:32.186 --> 00:07:32.826
It really does.

00:07:32.826 --> 00:07:34.106
It's a huge achievement.

00:07:34.106 --> 00:07:35.786
Here's where it gets really interesting, though.

00:07:36.746 --> 00:07:40.746
How does an incredibly pristine compliance record like that

00:07:40.746 --> 00:07:44.986
actually change the tone when Puerto Rico walks into the halls of Congress?

00:07:44.986 --> 00:07:47.146
Well, it shifts the entire power dynamic.

00:07:47.146 --> 00:07:47.706
How so?

00:07:47.706 --> 00:07:52.586
When you walk into a congressional committee with a 2.51% error rate,

00:07:52.586 --> 00:07:57.066
you are no longer asking for an emergency favor or a temporary bailout.

00:07:57.066 --> 00:07:58.346
Right, you're not desperate.

00:07:58.346 --> 00:07:59.306
Exactly.

00:07:59.306 --> 00:08:02.506
You are arriving with hard, undeniable data proving

00:08:02.506 --> 00:08:05.946
that you are a highly responsible, efficient steward of federal funds.

00:08:05.946 --> 00:08:06.746
They did the homework.

00:08:06.746 --> 00:08:09.866
They built the infrastructure, they implemented the strict oversight,

00:08:09.866 --> 00:08:12.106
and they executed the federal mandate.

00:08:12.106 --> 00:08:14.346
It allows them to demand structural parity,

00:08:14.346 --> 00:08:15.626
not just because they need it,

00:08:15.626 --> 00:08:18.346
but because they have proven they can manage it.

00:08:18.346 --> 00:08:21.866
Okay, so a pristine compliance record is obviously a phenomenal tool.

00:08:22.666 --> 00:08:24.746
But to actually sell it to Congress,

00:08:24.746 --> 00:08:27.226
Puerto Rico cannot afford to have mixed messaging.

00:08:27.226 --> 00:08:28.346
No, definitely not.

00:08:28.346 --> 00:08:30.586
Because if you have five different lobbying groups

00:08:30.586 --> 00:08:34.266
whispering five different sets of numbers into the ears of lawmakers,

00:08:34.266 --> 00:08:35.386
the whole thing just falls apart.

00:08:35.386 --> 00:08:36.746
It would be a disaster, yeah.

00:08:36.746 --> 00:08:39.546
Which brings us to this massive strategic move,

00:08:39.546 --> 00:08:41.066
the governor's executive order.

00:08:41.946 --> 00:08:43.786
In February 2026,

00:08:43.786 --> 00:08:48.106
the governor signed executive order 2026-006

00:08:48.106 --> 00:08:50.906
to establish a multi-sectoral working group.

00:08:50.906 --> 00:08:53.946
And honestly, the composition of that working group

00:08:53.946 --> 00:08:56.506
is a masterclass in political strategy.

00:08:56.506 --> 00:08:56.986
Really?

00:08:56.986 --> 00:08:57.626
Why is that?

00:08:58.346 --> 00:09:00.986
Because it unites incredibly diverse factions

00:09:00.986 --> 00:09:03.306
that naturally have conflicting interests.

00:09:03.306 --> 00:09:04.986
You have the Department of Health,

00:09:04.986 --> 00:09:05.946
you have ASIS,

00:09:05.946 --> 00:09:08.106
which is the Island's Health Insurance Administration.

00:09:08.106 --> 00:09:09.146
What did the government say?

00:09:09.146 --> 00:09:09.466
Right.

00:09:09.466 --> 00:09:11.226
And you have AAFAF,

00:09:11.226 --> 00:09:13.946
the Fiscal Agency Managing the Government's Finances.

00:09:13.946 --> 00:09:16.506
You also have PRFAA,

00:09:16.506 --> 00:09:18.746
Puerto Rico's official voice in Washington,

00:09:18.746 --> 00:09:20.506
alongside the resident commissioner.

00:09:20.506 --> 00:09:21.946
So all the key political players.

00:09:21.946 --> 00:09:24.586
But crucially, they brought in the private sector too.

00:09:24.586 --> 00:09:26.106
The hospital association,

00:09:26.106 --> 00:09:27.066
the pharmacies,

00:09:27.066 --> 00:09:29.626
and the 330 community health centers.

00:09:29.626 --> 00:09:31.706
Hold on, let's clarify that last one for the listener.

00:09:31.706 --> 00:09:34.426
When we say 330 community health centers,

00:09:34.986 --> 00:09:38.586
we are talking about federally qualified health clinics

00:09:38.586 --> 00:09:40.506
that serve the most vulnerable

00:09:40.506 --> 00:09:42.346
low-income populations on the island.

00:09:42.906 --> 00:09:43.706
Precisely.

00:09:43.706 --> 00:09:46.586
They are the absolute front line of healthcare

00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:48.266
for hundreds of thousands of people.

00:09:48.986 --> 00:09:51.466
So getting everyone at the same table is a big deal.

00:09:51.466 --> 00:09:54.186
The operative duty of bringing all these heavy hitters

00:09:54.186 --> 00:09:58.026
into one room is to design one unified strategy.

00:09:58.026 --> 00:10:00.986
They call it the One Voice Mandate, or Unes Olivos.

00:10:00.986 --> 00:10:03.066
But let me just play devil's advocate for a second here.

00:10:03.066 --> 00:10:03.466
Sure.

00:10:03.466 --> 00:10:06.426
Getting hospitals who naturally want higher payouts,

00:10:06.426 --> 00:10:08.906
pharmacies who want better dispensing fees,

00:10:08.906 --> 00:10:11.306
and government agencies trying to balance a budget

00:10:11.306 --> 00:10:14.026
to all agree on a single legislative strategy

00:10:14.666 --> 00:10:16.186
is notoriously difficult.

00:10:16.746 --> 00:10:18.586
How do they actually lock this in?

00:10:18.586 --> 00:10:20.906
Well, by recognizing that the alternative

00:10:20.906 --> 00:10:22.746
is mutual destruction, basically.

00:10:22.746 --> 00:10:23.706
Mutual destruction.

00:10:23.706 --> 00:10:26.346
Yeah, fragmented lobbying is guaranteed to fail,

00:10:26.346 --> 00:10:28.106
and all the stakeholders know it.

00:10:28.106 --> 00:10:30.426
If factions from Puerto Rico go to Washington

00:10:30.426 --> 00:10:31.946
asking for different things,

00:10:31.946 --> 00:10:34.106
Congress will just simply exploit those divisions.

00:10:34.106 --> 00:10:34.666
Oh, I see.

00:10:34.666 --> 00:10:36.186
Lawmakers will throw their hands up,

00:10:36.186 --> 00:10:38.426
say Puerto Rico doesn't even know what it wants,

00:10:38.426 --> 00:10:41.386
and they'll just pass another temporary inadequate patch

00:10:41.386 --> 00:10:42.986
instead of a permanent solution.

00:10:42.986 --> 00:10:45.866
So the executive order practically forces them to align.

00:10:45.866 --> 00:10:46.826
Exactly.

00:10:46.826 --> 00:10:48.586
It legally binds the strategy.

00:10:49.466 --> 00:10:50.986
Through this executive order,

00:10:50.986 --> 00:10:53.306
and a subsequent memorandum of understanding

00:10:53.306 --> 00:10:57.066
in May 2026, the official ask is locked.

00:10:57.706 --> 00:10:58.746
No deviations.

00:10:58.746 --> 00:11:00.266
Absolutely no freelancing,

00:11:00.266 --> 00:11:01.546
different numbers in Washington.

00:11:02.106 --> 00:11:04.026
The unified demand is set in stone,

00:11:04.666 --> 00:11:09.466
a permanent 83% FMAP, and $4.415 billion

00:11:09.466 --> 00:11:11.066
for fiscal year 2028,

00:11:11.066 --> 00:11:13.466
with a 5% annual growth rate built in.

00:11:13.466 --> 00:11:14.506
Buna solavos.

00:11:14.506 --> 00:11:17.786
I mean, it is incredibly smart defensive politics.

00:11:17.786 --> 00:11:19.626
It really is the only way forward.

00:11:19.626 --> 00:11:21.626
But we've been throwing around billions of dollars

00:11:21.626 --> 00:11:23.546
acronyms in high-level working groups.

00:11:23.546 --> 00:11:25.546
We need to ground this in the human reality.

00:11:25.546 --> 00:11:26.346
Definitely.

00:11:26.346 --> 00:11:28.186
So what does this all mean for you, the listener?

00:11:28.186 --> 00:11:31.306
Why is this specific funding battle so vital

00:11:31.306 --> 00:11:32.746
to the human beings relying on it?

00:11:33.306 --> 00:11:35.066
To really understand the human cost,

00:11:35.066 --> 00:11:36.506
you have to look at the health profile

00:11:36.506 --> 00:11:39.066
of the 1.3 million people covered by Plan Vital.

00:11:39.066 --> 00:11:40.186
What do the numbers look like?

00:11:40.186 --> 00:11:42.026
According to the roadmap data,

00:11:42.026 --> 00:11:45.226
between 60% and 70% of that population

00:11:45.226 --> 00:11:47.546
has at least one critical health condition.

00:11:47.546 --> 00:11:49.146
That is a staggering majority.

00:11:49.146 --> 00:11:49.786
It is.

00:11:49.786 --> 00:11:52.026
And the governor's executive order explicitly

00:11:52.026 --> 00:11:55.066
highlights a major driver of the inequality they suffer.

00:11:55.706 --> 00:11:58.906
Puerto Rico is entirely excluded from federal programs

00:11:58.906 --> 00:12:02.266
like supplemental security income, extra help,

00:12:02.266 --> 00:12:06.506
and crucially, the Medicare Savings Program, or the MSP.

00:12:06.506 --> 00:12:07.866
Okay, let's break down the MSP,

00:12:07.866 --> 00:12:09.386
because this is where the inequality

00:12:09.386 --> 00:12:10.826
gets really, really tangible.

00:12:10.826 --> 00:12:11.786
Yeah, let's do it.

00:12:11.786 --> 00:12:12.906
The Medicare Savings Program

00:12:12.906 --> 00:12:14.826
is a federal initiative on the mainland

00:12:14.826 --> 00:12:16.986
that helps low-income individuals pay

00:12:16.986 --> 00:12:19.546
for their Medicare premiums and cost sharing.

00:12:19.546 --> 00:12:22.746
And because Puerto Rico is excluded from it,

00:12:22.746 --> 00:12:24.506
the cost doesn't just evaporate,

00:12:24.506 --> 00:12:26.906
it creates a disproportionate fiscal burden

00:12:26.906 --> 00:12:28.666
placed directly on the local government.

00:12:28.666 --> 00:12:29.866
Right, somebody still has to pay.

00:12:29.866 --> 00:12:30.666
Exactly.

00:12:30.666 --> 00:12:32.426
When the local government has to absorb

00:12:32.426 --> 00:12:35.226
that massive cost just to keep the system afloat,

00:12:35.226 --> 00:12:36.666
it strictly limits their ability

00:12:36.666 --> 00:12:39.066
to expand the government health plan

00:12:39.066 --> 00:12:42.106
or offer better, more comprehensive services.

00:12:42.106 --> 00:12:43.226
So for a listener,

00:12:43.226 --> 00:12:46.426
this means an elderly grandparent on the island

00:12:46.426 --> 00:12:49.306
is forced to choose between paying for groceries

00:12:49.306 --> 00:12:51.226
and paying their Medicare premium

00:12:51.226 --> 00:12:54.026
because that federal help just isn't there.

00:12:54.026 --> 00:12:56.746
That's the tragic reality for many, yes.

00:12:56.746 --> 00:13:00.266
The local system is just strained to the breaking point,

00:13:00.266 --> 00:13:02.906
and the most glaring evidence of this strain

00:13:02.906 --> 00:13:04.426
is medical migration, right?

00:13:04.426 --> 00:13:05.306
Absolutely.

00:13:05.306 --> 00:13:08.106
The sources reveal a pretty stark reality.

00:13:08.106 --> 00:13:11.706
33% of Puerto Ricans who migrate to the U.S. mainland

00:13:11.706 --> 00:13:15.306
do so specifically seeking better healthcare services.

00:13:15.306 --> 00:13:16.186
Think about that.

00:13:16.186 --> 00:13:18.426
That is a profound demographic shift

00:13:18.426 --> 00:13:21.226
driven entirely by unequal policy.

00:13:21.226 --> 00:13:22.826
People are literally leaving their homes,

00:13:22.826 --> 00:13:24.106
their families, and their culture

00:13:24.106 --> 00:13:25.546
just to get basic care.

00:13:25.546 --> 00:13:26.586
It's heartbreaking,

00:13:26.586 --> 00:13:28.266
and here is the staggering irony

00:13:28.266 --> 00:13:29.546
that the roadmap points out.

00:13:30.186 --> 00:13:33.066
When those individuals are forced to move to the mainland,

00:13:33.066 --> 00:13:34.186
the U.S. federal government

00:13:34.186 --> 00:13:36.026
ends up paying for their care anyway.

00:13:36.026 --> 00:13:38.506
Yes, but at a vastly higher cost.

00:13:38.506 --> 00:13:38.906
Right.

00:13:38.906 --> 00:13:40.906
Providing those healthcare services on the mainland

00:13:40.906 --> 00:13:42.026
costs the U.S. government

00:13:42.026 --> 00:13:44.346
roughly $165 million annually.

00:13:44.346 --> 00:13:45.386
And if they stayed?

00:13:45.386 --> 00:13:47.786
If they provided equitable funding

00:13:47.786 --> 00:13:50.666
so those same citizens could receive care on the island,

00:13:51.306 --> 00:13:53.226
it would only cost $44 million.

00:13:53.866 --> 00:13:55.546
It literally costs the federal government

00:13:55.546 --> 00:13:56.986
nearly four times as much

00:13:56.986 --> 00:13:59.066
to fund the symptom of the problem

00:13:59.066 --> 00:14:01.226
rather than just fixing the root cause.

00:14:01.226 --> 00:14:02.506
It's completely baffling.

00:14:02.506 --> 00:14:02.906
It is.

00:14:03.466 --> 00:14:06.906
This inequality actively drives medical migration,

00:14:06.906 --> 00:14:09.706
and it is draining federal resources in the long run.

00:14:09.706 --> 00:14:11.066
And that is exactly why

00:14:11.066 --> 00:14:12.666
this upcoming legislative push

00:14:12.666 --> 00:14:14.346
is the most critical hurdle they face.

00:14:14.346 --> 00:14:15.466
All right, it's do or die.

00:14:16.026 --> 00:14:17.546
The stakes are massive.

00:14:17.546 --> 00:14:19.866
The one voice ask is locked in,

00:14:19.866 --> 00:14:22.426
and the homework regarding compliance is totally done.

00:14:22.986 --> 00:14:24.666
So now we have to look at the exact

00:14:24.666 --> 00:14:26.506
step-by-step legislative counter.

00:14:26.506 --> 00:14:27.786
A 12-month countdown.

00:14:27.786 --> 00:14:28.426
Exactly.

00:14:28.426 --> 00:14:30.266
We have a strict 12-month window

00:14:30.266 --> 00:14:31.786
to execute this roadmap

00:14:31.786 --> 00:14:34.186
and stabilize Plan Vital permanently.

00:14:34.186 --> 00:14:36.426
So let's slow down and walk through this timeline

00:14:36.426 --> 00:14:39.386
starting right now through the fall of 2026.

00:14:39.386 --> 00:14:41.066
Okay, so the fall of 2026

00:14:41.066 --> 00:14:42.826
is entirely about positioning.

00:14:42.826 --> 00:14:43.786
Setting the stage.

00:14:43.786 --> 00:14:44.426
Right.

00:14:44.426 --> 00:14:45.706
Puerto Rico must confirm

00:14:45.706 --> 00:14:48.586
the fiscal year 2026 certifications,

00:14:48.586 --> 00:14:51.866
that's the $300 million and $75 million

00:14:51.866 --> 00:14:53.226
add-ons we discussed earlier,

00:14:53.866 --> 00:14:56.426
and file the required annual report.

00:14:56.426 --> 00:14:57.706
Check the boxes, basically.

00:14:57.706 --> 00:14:58.266
Exactly.

00:14:58.906 --> 00:15:02.106
Then they must deliver their unified two-page ask

00:15:02.106 --> 00:15:03.466
along with Alison Mitchell's

00:15:03.466 --> 00:15:04.986
as CRS reports

00:15:04.986 --> 00:15:06.986
and the governor's executive order

00:15:06.986 --> 00:15:09.706
directly to the key congressional committees.

00:15:09.706 --> 00:15:11.466
Which committees are we talking about here?

00:15:11.466 --> 00:15:12.906
The Energy and Commerce Committee,

00:15:12.906 --> 00:15:14.266
the Finance Committee,

00:15:14.266 --> 00:15:15.306
and CMS.

00:15:15.306 --> 00:15:18.106
And there is a crucial tactical warning

00:15:18.106 --> 00:15:20.026
in the roadmap for this fall period.

00:15:20.906 --> 00:15:22.186
Do not hang this effort

00:15:22.186 --> 00:15:24.026
on the October continuing resolution.

00:15:24.026 --> 00:15:25.706
Yes, that is a huge point.

00:15:25.706 --> 00:15:27.066
Let's translate that for the listener

00:15:27.066 --> 00:15:27.546
really quick.

00:15:27.546 --> 00:15:29.466
A continuing resolution or a CR

00:15:29.466 --> 00:15:30.826
is a stop-get measure.

00:15:30.826 --> 00:15:32.746
It's when Congress can't agree on a budget,

00:15:32.746 --> 00:15:34.186
so they just pass a temporary bill

00:15:34.186 --> 00:15:35.226
to keep the government open

00:15:35.226 --> 00:15:36.266
for a few weeks or months.

00:15:36.266 --> 00:15:37.946
Right, just kicking the can down the road.

00:15:37.946 --> 00:15:38.746
Exactly.

00:15:38.746 --> 00:15:40.266
So why is that so dangerous

00:15:40.266 --> 00:15:41.706
for Puerto Rico's strategy?

00:15:41.706 --> 00:15:44.506
Because a CR is, by definition,

00:15:44.506 --> 00:15:45.466
a temporary patch.

00:15:46.106 --> 00:15:48.586
If you attach a permanent structural fix

00:15:48.586 --> 00:15:50.586
to a temporary funding vehicle,

00:15:50.586 --> 00:15:52.986
it gets stripped out during negotiations.

00:15:52.986 --> 00:15:53.946
Ah, I see.

00:15:53.946 --> 00:15:55.146
Lawmakers will just say,

00:15:55.146 --> 00:15:57.626
look, we're just keeping the lights on right now.

00:15:57.626 --> 00:15:59.306
We don't have time to debate

00:15:59.306 --> 00:16:00.906
permanent Medicaid parity.

00:16:00.906 --> 00:16:02.186
That makes total sense.

00:16:02.186 --> 00:16:03.946
The roadmap explicitly warns

00:16:03.946 --> 00:16:05.066
against using the CR

00:16:05.066 --> 00:16:06.506
because this requires

00:16:06.506 --> 00:16:09.386
a dedicated, focused legislative push,

00:16:09.386 --> 00:16:10.506
not an afterthought

00:16:10.506 --> 00:16:12.026
in a rushed spending bill.

00:16:12.026 --> 00:16:13.226
Keep it off the CR.

00:16:13.226 --> 00:16:13.946
Got it.

00:16:13.946 --> 00:16:15.546
Then we move into the winter phase.

00:16:15.546 --> 00:16:19.626
October 2026 through January 2027.

00:16:19.626 --> 00:16:21.386
Right, the next critical window.

00:16:21.386 --> 00:16:23.946
The goal here is to secure a discussion draft

00:16:23.946 --> 00:16:25.066
before Thanksgiving.

00:16:25.066 --> 00:16:26.506
And this is where the optics

00:16:26.506 --> 00:16:27.946
of the One Voice mandate

00:16:27.946 --> 00:16:29.386
really come into play.

00:16:29.386 --> 00:16:31.146
The governor and the resident commissioner

00:16:31.146 --> 00:16:32.986
must appear together in Washington

00:16:32.986 --> 00:16:36.106
to present the exact same unified numbers.

00:16:36.106 --> 00:16:38.586
That united front is absolutely essential.

00:16:38.586 --> 00:16:39.946
And during this winter phase,

00:16:39.946 --> 00:16:42.746
they will also attempt to build a lame duck bridge.

00:16:42.746 --> 00:16:43.946
A lame duck bridge.

00:16:43.946 --> 00:16:46.426
Meaning they are trying to get a bridge agreement

00:16:46.426 --> 00:16:49.146
passed during that weird chaotic period

00:16:49.146 --> 00:16:50.586
after the November election,

00:16:50.586 --> 00:16:52.426
but before the newly elected Congress

00:16:52.426 --> 00:16:54.666
is actually sworn in during January.

00:16:54.666 --> 00:16:55.146
Exactly.

00:16:55.146 --> 00:16:56.186
That lame duck session

00:16:56.186 --> 00:16:57.786
is notoriously unpredictable.

00:16:57.786 --> 00:16:58.426
Oh, I'm sure.

00:16:58.426 --> 00:17:00.506
Which is why having a unified front

00:17:00.506 --> 00:17:02.346
is the only way to cut through the noise.

00:17:02.986 --> 00:17:06.026
And that brings us to the critical spring window.

00:17:06.826 --> 00:17:09.546
January through April 2027.

00:17:09.546 --> 00:17:10.506
What happens in the spring?

00:17:11.386 --> 00:17:14.586
Well, if we connect this to the bigger picture,

00:17:14.586 --> 00:17:16.506
legislative strategy at this stage

00:17:16.506 --> 00:17:17.946
is entirely calendar.

00:17:17.946 --> 00:17:18.746
Meaning?

00:17:18.746 --> 00:17:20.906
It is no longer about designing the policy.

00:17:20.906 --> 00:17:22.986
It is about pure political execution

00:17:22.986 --> 00:17:24.346
against federal deadlines.

00:17:24.346 --> 00:17:24.826
Right.

00:17:24.826 --> 00:17:26.666
This is the season for markups

00:17:26.666 --> 00:17:27.786
and committee hearings.

00:17:27.786 --> 00:17:29.306
And most importantly,

00:17:29.306 --> 00:17:31.466
getting the congressional budget office,

00:17:31.466 --> 00:17:33.706
the CBO, to score the different tiers

00:17:33.706 --> 00:17:34.506
of the proposal.

00:17:34.506 --> 00:17:36.106
Right, CBO scoring.

00:17:36.106 --> 00:17:38.186
That's when the nonpartisan accountants

00:17:38.186 --> 00:17:40.186
tell Congress exactly how much a bill

00:17:40.186 --> 00:17:42.026
is going to cost over the next 10 years.

00:17:42.026 --> 00:17:43.146
The moment of truth.

00:17:43.146 --> 00:17:45.546
It is often the graveyard of good legislation

00:17:45.546 --> 00:17:46.986
if the score comes back too high.

00:17:46.986 --> 00:17:49.466
Which is why a very specific lever

00:17:49.466 --> 00:17:51.466
needs to be pulled during the spring window.

00:17:51.466 --> 00:17:52.426
OK, what kind of lever?

00:17:52.426 --> 00:17:54.506
A formal letter from the financial oversight

00:17:54.506 --> 00:17:57.146
and management board, the FOMB, is required.

00:17:57.146 --> 00:17:58.266
The oversight board.

00:17:58.266 --> 00:17:58.586
Yes.

00:17:59.146 --> 00:18:02.506
The FOMB must formally warn Congress

00:18:02.506 --> 00:18:05.946
that a $3.5 billion cut to Medicaid

00:18:06.026 --> 00:18:08.026
would fundamentally break the already

00:18:08.026 --> 00:18:09.546
certified budgets of the island.

00:18:09.546 --> 00:18:11.866
Wow, that's the ultimate alarm bell.

00:18:11.866 --> 00:18:13.946
You have the official fiscal watchdogs

00:18:13.946 --> 00:18:16.906
telling Congress, if you let this cliff happen,

00:18:16.906 --> 00:18:19.146
you blow up the entire financial stability

00:18:19.146 --> 00:18:20.426
we've spent years building.

00:18:20.426 --> 00:18:21.466
Exactly.

00:18:21.466 --> 00:18:22.826
It turns a healthcare issue

00:18:22.826 --> 00:18:25.386
into a catastrophic macroeconomic issue.

00:18:25.386 --> 00:18:26.906
Which creates immense pressure

00:18:26.906 --> 00:18:28.586
as we enter the summer sprint.

00:18:28.586 --> 00:18:31.146
May through September 2027.

00:18:31.146 --> 00:18:32.506
The final countdown.

00:18:32.506 --> 00:18:34.266
The roadmap is very clear

00:18:34.266 --> 00:18:35.946
about the fallback strategy here.

00:18:35.946 --> 00:18:38.906
The primary goal is tier A structural parity,

00:18:38.906 --> 00:18:40.586
meaning an open-ended funding system

00:18:40.586 --> 00:18:42.026
just like the 50 states have.

00:18:42.026 --> 00:18:43.546
That's the dream scenario.

00:18:43.546 --> 00:18:44.186
Right.

00:18:44.186 --> 00:18:45.866
But if there is no standalone bill

00:18:45.866 --> 00:18:47.226
passed by Memorial Day,

00:18:47.226 --> 00:18:49.066
the strategy must immediately shift.

00:18:49.066 --> 00:18:50.266
They have to attach a rider

00:18:50.266 --> 00:18:53.146
to a must pass September legislative vehicle.

00:18:53.146 --> 00:18:54.026
A fullback plan.

00:18:54.026 --> 00:18:55.546
Let's clarify that for the listener.

00:18:55.546 --> 00:18:55.706
Yeah.

00:18:55.706 --> 00:18:57.546
Attaching a rider to a must pass vehicle

00:18:57.546 --> 00:18:58.906
means taking the Medicaid fix

00:18:58.906 --> 00:19:00.506
and just hiding it inside a giant

00:19:00.506 --> 00:19:01.946
unrelated bill that Congress

00:19:01.946 --> 00:19:03.626
absolutely has to vote yes on.

00:19:03.626 --> 00:19:05.306
Like a massive defense spending bill

00:19:05.306 --> 00:19:07.466
or a national disaster relief package.

00:19:07.466 --> 00:19:08.346
Exactly.

00:19:08.346 --> 00:19:09.706
You attach this so it gets dragged

00:19:09.706 --> 00:19:10.826
across the finish line

00:19:10.826 --> 00:19:12.586
whether they want to debate it or not.

00:19:12.586 --> 00:19:14.586
It is the ultimate pragmatic maneuver.

00:19:15.306 --> 00:19:17.306
And the roadmap actually defines

00:19:17.306 --> 00:19:20.186
the absolute minimum acceptable fallback

00:19:20.186 --> 00:19:20.986
for that rider.

00:19:20.986 --> 00:19:22.186
What's the minimum?

00:19:22.186 --> 00:19:23.866
They must freeze the fiscal year

00:19:23.866 --> 00:19:25.386
2027 funding

00:19:25.386 --> 00:19:27.546
plus the medical consumer price index.

00:19:27.546 --> 00:19:29.466
And they must freeze the FMAP

00:19:29.466 --> 00:19:32.346
at either 76% or 83%

00:19:32.346 --> 00:19:33.706
for at least two years.

00:19:33.706 --> 00:19:35.066
Just to stop the bleeding.

00:19:35.066 --> 00:19:35.786
Exactly.

00:19:35.786 --> 00:19:37.226
That is the safety net to prevent

00:19:37.226 --> 00:19:38.426
a total collapse

00:19:38.426 --> 00:19:40.186
if full structural parity

00:19:40.186 --> 00:19:42.026
cannot get through as a standalone bill.

00:19:42.026 --> 00:19:42.266
Okay.

00:19:42.266 --> 00:19:43.706
So let's bring this all together.

00:19:44.266 --> 00:19:46.906
Puerto Rico has completely transformed

00:19:46.906 --> 00:19:47.946
its Medicaid narrative

00:19:47.946 --> 00:19:48.906
over the last few years.

00:19:48.906 --> 00:19:49.706
They really have.

00:19:49.706 --> 00:19:51.226
They aren't walking into this 12 month

00:19:51.226 --> 00:19:52.746
window empty handed.

00:19:52.746 --> 00:19:54.426
They have the undeniable

00:19:54.426 --> 00:19:55.626
nonpartisan facts

00:19:55.626 --> 00:19:57.866
courtesy of Alison Mitchell's CRS reports.

00:19:57.866 --> 00:19:59.066
The baseline is set.

00:19:59.066 --> 00:20:01.146
They have a stellar proven compliance record

00:20:01.146 --> 00:20:02.826
with the CAA of 2023.

00:20:03.466 --> 00:20:06.666
I mean, remember that incredible 2.51% error rate?

00:20:06.666 --> 00:20:08.026
Hard to forget that one.

00:20:08.026 --> 00:20:10.746
And thanks to the governor's executive order,

00:20:10.746 --> 00:20:14.026
they have a unified one voice mandate.

00:20:14.026 --> 00:20:15.146
Gunas Olivos.

00:20:15.146 --> 00:20:15.706
Exactly.

00:20:16.266 --> 00:20:18.026
The remaining work for the next 12 months

00:20:18.026 --> 00:20:20.026
isn't about figuring out what to ask for.

00:20:20.026 --> 00:20:21.626
It is purely about

00:20:21.626 --> 00:20:24.106
political execution on a very tight

00:20:24.106 --> 00:20:26.266
unforgiving federal calendar.

00:20:26.266 --> 00:20:27.866
And the message to Congress

00:20:27.866 --> 00:20:29.626
drawn directly from this roadmap

00:20:29.626 --> 00:20:31.226
is firm and clear.

00:20:31.226 --> 00:20:32.266
What's the bottom line?

00:20:32.266 --> 00:20:34.106
The island has done the homework

00:20:34.106 --> 00:20:36.586
that Congress imposed in 2022.

00:20:36.586 --> 00:20:37.786
They met the standards,

00:20:37.786 --> 00:20:39.626
they improved the administrative integrity

00:20:39.626 --> 00:20:40.666
of the system,

00:20:40.666 --> 00:20:41.946
and they proved they can manage

00:20:41.946 --> 00:20:43.146
these funds efficiently.

00:20:43.146 --> 00:20:44.266
They showed the receipts.

00:20:44.266 --> 00:20:44.906
They did.

00:20:44.906 --> 00:20:46.746
It is now entirely on Congress

00:20:46.746 --> 00:20:48.666
to recognize this compliance.

00:20:48.666 --> 00:20:49.866
It is time for Congress

00:20:49.866 --> 00:20:52.426
to enact tier A structural parity

00:20:52.426 --> 00:20:54.666
to prevent a disastrous and costly

00:20:54.666 --> 00:20:55.466
fiscal cliff

00:20:55.466 --> 00:20:56.746
that will ultimately cost

00:20:56.746 --> 00:20:57.546
the federal government

00:20:57.546 --> 00:20:58.746
more money in the long run.

00:20:58.826 --> 00:21:00.346
It's completely in their hands now.

00:21:00.906 --> 00:21:02.586
We've covered the billions of dollars,

00:21:02.586 --> 00:21:04.346
the intricate legislative strategy,

00:21:04.346 --> 00:21:06.346
and the 12 month ticking clock.

00:21:06.906 --> 00:21:08.586
But I want to leave you with a final thought

00:21:08.586 --> 00:21:09.226
to ponder

00:21:09.866 --> 00:21:11.706
something that goes beyond the spreadsheets

00:21:11.706 --> 00:21:12.986
and the congressional hearings.

00:21:12.986 --> 00:21:14.186
Always good to zoom out.

00:21:14.186 --> 00:21:17.066
If Puerto Rico successfully achieves

00:21:17.066 --> 00:21:19.146
this state-like open-ended funding

00:21:19.146 --> 00:21:20.186
in the next 12 months,

00:21:21.146 --> 00:21:23.386
how might this unprecedented shift

00:21:23.386 --> 00:21:25.546
in health equity begin to reverse

00:21:25.546 --> 00:21:27.946
the decades-long demographic trend

00:21:27.946 --> 00:21:29.946
of medical migration to the mainland?

00:21:29.946 --> 00:21:30.986
That's the real question.

00:21:30.986 --> 00:21:31.466
Right.

00:21:31.466 --> 00:21:32.506
If the healthcare system

00:21:32.506 --> 00:21:33.946
is finally made whole

00:21:33.946 --> 00:21:34.906
and elderly patients

00:21:34.906 --> 00:21:36.346
don't have to choose between groceries

00:21:36.346 --> 00:21:37.786
and their Medicare premiums,

00:21:37.786 --> 00:21:38.586
how many families

00:21:38.586 --> 00:21:40.106
will finally be able to stay home?

00:21:40.106 --> 00:21:41.226
Thank you so much for joining us

00:21:41.226 --> 00:21:42.266
on this deep dive.

00:21:42.266 --> 00:21:43.626
We encourage you to keep exploring

00:21:43.626 --> 00:21:44.346
the material

